        {
          "id": "0530ffa7-87ae-30e4-9640-ae94fcee45c1",
          "content": {
            "id": "0530ffa7-87ae-30e4-9640-ae94fcee45c1",
            "contentType": "VIDEO",
            "title": "Japan stocks soar after Takaichi elected as ruling party leader",
            "description": "<title><body><p>STORY: Japanese stocks soared Monday after Sanae Takaichi came out on top to lead Japan's ruling party.</p><p>That makes her set to become the country's first female prime minister, subject to confirmation by lawmakers.\u00a0\u00a0</p><p>The Nikkei surged close to 5% to hit a new record high.</p><p>It passed the 48,000-mark for the first time, as analysts broadly welcomed her commitment to economic stimulus.\u00a0</p><p>Defense contractor Mitsubishi Heavy Industries was among the top movers, jumping around 11% as more military spending is expected under Takaichi.</p><p>Tech stocks also surged, with chip gear maker Advantest jumping 14%.\u00a0</p><p>:: October 4, 2025</p><p>The veteran lawmaker won Saturday's run-off vote in the Liberal Democratic Party race.</p><p>Upon victory, Takaichi made clear her priority would be to revive demand and the broader market.\u00a0</p><p>Known to be an advocate of big fiscal spending, she is a devotee of the stimulus policies developed by her late predecessor Shinzo Abe.\u00a0</p><p>Takaichi has called for tax cuts to cushion the rising cost of living.</p><p>And she's criticized the Bank of Japan's decision to raise interest rates, dimming bets rates will be hiked at its meeting at the end of October.</p><p>That saw the yen sink, trading at 150 yen against the dollar.</p><p>Longer-term Japanese government bonds also fell, on the expectations Takaichi will push for more spending.</p></body></title>",
            "summary": "<body><p>STORY: Japanese stocks soared Monday after Sanae Takaichi came out on top to lead Japan's ruling party.</p><p>That makes her set to become the country's first female prime minister, subject to confirmation by lawmakers.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p>The Nikkei surged close to 5% to hit a new record high.</p><p>It passed the 48,000-mark for the first time, as analysts broadly welcomed her commitment to economic stimulus.&nbsp;</p><p>Defense contractor Mitsubishi Heavy Industries was among the top movers, jumping around 11% as more military spending is expected under Takaichi.</p><p>Tech stocks also surged, with chip gear maker Advantest jumping 14%.&nbsp;</p><p>:: October 4, 2025</p><p>The veteran lawmaker won Saturday's run-off vote in the Liberal Democratic Party race.</p><p>Upon victory, Takaichi made clear her priority would be to revive demand and the broader market.&nbsp;</p><p>Known to be an advocate of big fiscal spending, she is a devotee of the stimulus policies developed by her late predecessor Shinzo Abe.&nbsp;</p><p>Takaichi has called for tax cuts to cushion the rising cost of living.</p><p>And she's criticized the Bank of Japan's decision to raise interest rates, dimming bets rates will be hiked at its meeting at the end of October.</p><p>That saw the yen sink, trading at 150 yen against the dollar.</p><p>Longer-term Japanese government bonds also fell, on the expectations Takaichi will push for more spending.</p></body>",
            "pubDate": "2025-10-06T08:49:05Z",
            "displayTime": "",
            "isHosted": true,
            "bypassModal": false,
